-- DICT-SEMANTICS §4 `gen-rec`, the DISCRIMINATING form of its inferred-context
-- twin: a mutually-recursive group in which only ONE body dispatches on the
-- class. `oddSz`'s body never mentions an `Sz` method, so it can acquire the
-- predicate only from the GROUP.
--
-- 🚨 PINNED DIVERGENCE -- ⚠️ UNFILED. Found 2026-07-30 while removing an inert
-- claim from `s4-gen-rec-inferred-context.mdk` (adversarial-review finding F6:
-- that fixture had `sz w` in BOTH bodies, so it could not distinguish per-binding
-- from group sourcing). Making the discriminator real is what exposed this.
-- Severity as observed: **S1, loud breakage** -- `check` accepts and prints the
-- CORRECT schemes; NEITHER engine will then execute the program. No silent
-- wrongness: both failures are loud, located and name the missing dict.
--
-- SPEC ANSWER (hand-derived): §4 `gen-rec` says `P'` is "the predicates deferred
-- to the (mutually) RECURSIVE GROUP" and that the group shares "ONE `λ d̄.`
-- prefix"; every recursive occurrence is elaborated "to `(x d₁ … dₘ)`: the SAME
-- dict params, NOT a fresh entailment". So `oddSz` abstracts the group's `Sz a`
-- dict exactly like `evenSz`, and passes it straight through on the cross-call.
--   SCHEMES: evenSz : Sz a => a -> Int -> Bool
--            oddSz  : Sz a => a -> Int -> Bool
--   VALUES:  `sz w` = 1 at `w = (1 : Int)` and `oddSz` steps by the literal 1,
--            so both arms decrement by one:                    => True, True
--
-- OBSERVED on this binary:
--   check  ACCEPT (exit 0) -- and it reports BOTH schemes correctly, i.e. the
--          group-wide `P'` attribution in §4 `gen-rec` is right at the TYPE level
--   run    REJECT -- `runtime error [E-PANIC]: unbound identifier: $dict_evenSz_0`
--   build  REJECT -- `error: emitter failed compiling …`
--          `runtime error [E-PANIC]: unbound dict witness '$dict_…__evenSz_0' in
--           emit env (dict not threaded to this site)`
--
-- So the SCHEME says the group shares a dict prefix and the ELABORATION does not
-- thread it: exactly §4's named failure mode ("a recursive call site that
-- re-enters entailment instead of passing `d₁…dₘ`", §10's `gen-rec` bullet),
-- caught one step earlier than a wrong value.
--
-- 🔬 TWO CONTROLS, both in the corpus, isolating the trigger to
-- INFERRED + MUTUAL + ASYMMETRIC:
--   * `s4-gen-rec-shared-dict-params.mdk` -- the same asymmetry is irrelevant
--     once the context is ASCRIBED; a signed group runs fine. (Probed directly
--     too: adding explicit `Sz a =>` signatures to THIS program makes it print
--     True/True on both engines.)
--   * `s4-gen-rec-inferred-context.mdk` -- the same INFERRED group with BOTH
--     bodies dispatching runs fine. So neither "inferred" nor "mutual" alone is
--     the trigger; the asymmetry is.
-- MIRROR-CHECKED: swapping which body dispatches fails symmetrically, naming
-- `$dict_oddSz_0` instead -- so this is not direction-dependent.
--
-- ⚠️ THIS ROW PINS A BROKEN BUILD ON PURPOSE. It goes RED the day either engine
-- starts executing it, which is the signal to re-pin it to ALL_EXACT `True\nTrue`
-- and close the issue this becomes.
interface Sz a where
  sz : a -> Int

impl Sz Int where
  sz n = n

evenSz w n = if n == 0 then True else oddSz w (n - sz w)

oddSz w n = if n == 0 then False else evenSz w (n - 1)

main =
  println (evenSz (1 : Int) 6)
  println (oddSz (1 : Int) 7)
